CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ unique_ptr member holds its value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG must FAIL: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
instantiating 'std::__enable_if_t' with <FALSE
--
The original hard error (concretizing unique_ptr's =delete'd deleter
constructor template into enable_if_t<false>::type, [temp.inst]/2) is fixed;
the disallowed pattern guards against regression.  The subsequent value bugs
are fixed by a chain of standards fixes: inherited base default constructors
([class.inhctor.init]), defaulted move constructors initializing bases from an
xvalue ([class.copy.ctor]/15) with the base subobject type recorded to
disambiguate EBO hierarchies like std::tuple's, and defaulted copy/move
assignment operators elaborated memberwise with base move-assignment going
through the base's operator= ([class.copy.assign]/12).  assertion.2 is a
deliberate WRONG-must-FAIL non-vacuity check.
